-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/277adcb4-c706-4da3-9c72-32005ebf777d
-- title:
--   Algorithm 4.1 — with $\delta_{opt}=\mu_{opt}=0$ RSSD generates an infinite sequence
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal M$ be a smooth embedded submanifold of a finite-dimensional real inner product space $E$, $R$ a retraction on $\mathcal M$ (Definition 2.1), and $\tilde f(\cdot,\mu)$ continuously differentiable for every $\mu>0$. For every input $x_0\in\mathcal M$, $\delta_0>0$, $\mu_0>0$, $\beta\in(0,1)$, $\bar\alpha>0$, $\theta_\delta,\theta_\mu\in(0,1)$ and $\sigma\in(0,1)$, Algorithm 4.1 with $\delta_{opt}=\mu_{opt}=0$ is well defined for all iterations: there are sequences
--
--   $$
--   (x_\ell,\mu_\ell,\delta_\ell)_{\ell\in\mathbb N}
--   $$
--
--   forming a run of the algorithm.
--
--   This is what makes "an infinite sequence generated by Algorithm 4.1" in Proposition 4.2 and Theorem 4.3 a meaningful hypothesis.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--
--   - generalization: M ⊂ ℝⁿ (or a matrix space with ⟨ξ,η⟩ = Tr(ξᵀη)) → M ⊆ E, E any finite-dimensional real inner product space; reason: ℝⁿ and the matrix spaces with the trace inner product are instances, so the Lean statement specializes to the source's
--   - generalization: M a complete Riemannian submanifold embedded in ℝⁿ → M a smooth embedded submanifold of fixed dimension d (C^k coordinate-slice charts for every k), completeness not assumed; reason: completeness is not used in the argument; complete submanifolds are a special case
--   - generalization: f̃ a smoothing function of f (Definition 2.7) → only f̃(·, μ) continuously differentiable for every μ > 0; reason: the existence of the iterates uses only this property; every smoothing function has it
--   - reading: σ in (4.1) but not on the input line of Algorithm 4.1 → σ ∈ (0,1); reason: the remark on p.15 needs σ < 1, and σ ∈ (0,1) is the Armijo convention of the cited reference [2]
--   - reading: m is the smallest integer such that (4.1) holds → m the least natural number with (4.1); reason: Armijo convention of [2], t_ℓ = β^m ᾱ ≤ ᾱ; over ℤ a least element need not exist
--   - encoding: Algorithm 4.1 with δ_opt = μ_opt = 0, including the stopping test of line 4 → the stopping test is omitted from the run predicate; reason: μ_ℓ > 0 for all ℓ, so the test ‖η_ℓ‖ ≤ 0 and μ_ℓ ≤ 0 never fires and every run is infinite
-- source:
--   Zhang, Chen & Ma, A Riemannian Smoothing Steepest Descent Method for Non-Lipschitz Optimization on Submanifolds, arXiv:2104.04199v1, Algorithm 4.1 and its remark, p.15, "This guarantees that the line search step (4.1) is well defined"

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ProjLikeRetr_Retractor_IsSubmanifold
import Definitions.Def_ZORiem_RGD_rgrad
import Definitions.Def_RiemSmoothSD_IsSmoothRetraction
import Definitions.Def_RiemSmoothSD_RSSDRun
set_option autoImplicit false

namespace RiemSmoothSD

open ProjLikeRetr.Retractor ZORiem.RGD

/-- **Algorithm 4.1 with `δ_opt = μ_opt = 0` generates an infinite sequence** (Zhang, Chen & Ma,
arXiv:2104.04199v1, Algorithm 4.1 and its remark, p.15, "This guarantees that the line search step
(4.1) is well defined"). Let `M` be a smooth embedded submanifold of `E`, `R` a retraction
(Definition 2.1), and `f̃(·, μ)` continuously differentiable for every `μ > 0`. For every input
`x₀ ∈ M`, `δ₀ > 0`, `μ₀ > 0`, `β ∈ (0,1)`, `ᾱ > 0`, `θ_δ, θ_μ ∈ (0,1)` and `σ ∈ (0,1)`, there are
sequences `(x_ℓ, μ_ℓ, δ_ℓ)_{ℓ ∈ ℕ}` forming a run of Algorithm 4.1.

**Formalization Note** A run is `IsRSSDRun`: the stopping test of line 4 never fires when
`δ_opt = μ_opt = 0`, so every run is infinite. Only the `C¹` property of the smoothing function is
assumed. -/
theorem rssd_run_exists {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E]
    [FiniteDimensional ℝ E] {d : ℕ} {M : Set E} (hM : ∀ k : ℕ, IsSubmanifold k d M)
    {R : E → E → E} (hR : IsSmoothRetraction M R) {ftilde : E → ℝ → ℝ}
    (hC1 : ∀ μ : ℝ, 0 < μ → ContDiff ℝ 1 (fun z => ftilde z μ))
    {x₀ : E} {δ₀ μ₀ β αbar θδ θμ σ : ℝ} (hin : RSSDInput M x₀ δ₀ μ₀ β αbar θδ θμ σ) :
    ∃ (x : ℕ → E) (μ δ : ℕ → ℝ), IsRSSDRun M R ftilde x₀ δ₀ μ₀ β αbar θδ θμ σ x μ δ := by sorry

end RiemSmoothSD
